RSX_PERF_SPLIT=1: one [perf-split] line per second, the walker's time per flip in exclusive buckets that add up to the walk.

Purpose: fps-plan step 2 (docs/in-progress/fps-plan.md). The existing walker timers ([perf-walk], [perf-walk2], [perf-flush])
are gross: a ring-full flush inside the descriptor, vertex or pipeline region is counted in both that region and the flush,
so their sum cannot be compared with the frame. This patch adds a walk-owned clock that charges every tick of one walk to
exactly one bucket, switched at the SAME sites that already carry the LDW_T0/LDW_ADD pairs (one LDS_IN/LDS_OUT line beside
each); the gross timers are untouched and still feed the other lines.

Env flag: RSX_PERF_SPLIT=1, off by default. Off: g_rsx_split_on is 0 and each site is one load and a not-taken branch; no
getenv beyond the one cached in rsx_perf_enabled(). On, it implies RSX_PERF=1 (the reporter thread and the cumulative
counters), starts the RSX_PERF_GPU poller (for gpu=) and, unless RSX_PERF_PROF=A,B is set, keeps the [perf-walk] window
open for the whole run (so the older [perf-walk*] lines print too).

Lines (ms per flip over the period, flips = RSX_PERF_FLIPS; nothing is printed in a period without flips):
  [perf-split] t= flips= frame= walk= decode= tex= pso= desc= vplan= fill= rec= wb= rbwait= submit= park= other= gpu=
  [perf-split-w] t= flips= busy= bar=
  [perf-split-cost] t= flips= words= commands= reads=
frame = wall time per flip; walk = RSX_PERF_WALK_T (lock wait included); the twelve buckets from decode to other add up to
walk (tools/runtime/perf/test_perf_split.py checks it on a log). Buckets and what feeds them:
  decode  the FIFO method region (the base bucket between the METH_T start and end): method and state decode, a draw's
          pre/surface/post work, clears, M2MF and NV3089 copies, and any untimed gap in a draw. Everything inside it that has
          a bucket of its own is taken out.
  vplan   the vertex layout plan and fetch (VTX_T's first half: payload-cache lookup, remap, hoisted fetch)
  fill    the vertex/index copy into the upload rings (VTX_COPY_T)
  pso     get_pso (key, memo, cache find, a synchronous build), plus the walker's wait on the async build workers (BAR)
  tex     the texture loop (D_TEXLOOP_T: decode, hash, upload, binding); a ring-full flush inside it is submit
  desc    descriptor ring, barriers, RTV/DSV, tables (D_DESC_T)
  rec     recording to Draw*Instanced (D_REC_T)
  wb      readback write-back work (NV0039 / sync write-backs) minus the flush and fence wait inside them
  rbwait  the fence wait inside a LD_FLUSH_READBACK flush
  submit  every other flush (list close and execute, frame-slot wait, non-readback fence waits), ld_wait_fence waits and
          the present when the flip is walked; the queue back-pressure bucket
  park    the walker's wait for the guest's user-command pump (gcm_walker_user_command) and the wait for the FIFO lock
  other   what no bucket claims (FIFO word parse, jumps and calls, labels, flips); it is the clock's own bucket, not a
          subtraction, so a bucket missed by a site shows here as a rise and the sum check still holds
gpu = the RSX_PERF_GPU poller's last busy window scaled to the period ("na" before its first window). It is not a walker
bucket and is not in the sum.
Worker line: busy = async pipeline-build worker CPU per flip (compile + create); bar = the walker's wait on those workers
(also inside pso=). There is no other worker yet; step 3's workers add their time here.
Cost line (after KZ3 2203): method commands and their data words walked through the method region, and the clock reads
the split itself made, per flip; a read costs about one QueryPerformanceCounter, so reads x that cost is the split's own
overhead, and about one read per command (half of each decode-boundary read) sits inside decode.

The split's cost (after KZ3 patch 2203, "perf split sampled decode cost", Project Helghast; same author, same licence as
the other KZ3 patches in this series; adapted, not pasted, because 6091 has a different shape from KZ3's 2188):
  - 2203 part 3 applies: while the split is on, the [perf-walk2] per-command meth_t0 timer pair is skipped. Its end read
    and two locked adds sat inside decode on every command (phantom decode time); under the split [perf-walk2]'s meth
    figures read 0. Split off: the same code as before.
  - 2203 part 1 (decode_d timed on 1 word in 32, scaled, owed debits clamped) does not apply: 6091 has no per-word decode
    timer; decode is a base bucket switched twice per command, and sampling those switches would scale the sampled
    command's own read by 32 too, so decode's expected phantom would stay the same while one heavy command (an M2MF or
    NV3089 copy) could move 32x its time between decode and other.
  - 2203 part 2 (the RSX_O0 m3d counters as pass-local adds) does not apply: there are no RSX_O0 counters in KZ2.
  - Its [perf-split-cost] line is ported with reads= in place of timed_words= (no word is timed here).
The clock's arithmetic is in a new header, libs/video/rsx_perf_split.h (struct + timestamps in, no thread or clock calls),
so the host test runs the walker's own code.

Not attributed: (1) the idle time between walks (walker_wait, flip pacing, SPU parks that end a walk) is outside the walk by
construction: frame - walk. (2) A flush or fence wait that nests in a bucket is charged to submit/rbwait, never to the
enclosing bucket; texture-upload and descriptor-ring flushes therefore show in submit, not in tex/desc. (3) The clock
owner is the thread holding the FIFO lock; enters from any other thread (guest-thread flips, workers) are ignored. (4)
decode includes work this patch does not split (D_SURF_T, D_PRE_T, D_POST_T, clears, M2MF, NV3089): the gross
[perf-walk2] lines still hold those splits. (5) A draw that returns early inside an open bucket (device loss) is reset at
the next method boundary.

Origin: KZ2 original, written for the step-2 split proposed to KZ3 (same line format in both ports). Reuses the 2029/2045/
6063/6070/6075 timer sites, WALK_T, FLIPS and the 2051 GPU poller; adds no timers to the sites that exist beyond the clock.
Not live-run: the counters are checked on the host by tools/runtime/perf/test_perf_split.py (format and sums) and the
clock by tools/runtime/perf/test_perf_split_clock.sh (exact buckets on a virtual clock, per-command decode, the cost
counters; negative controls: the meth pair kept, a lost charge); the walker itself needs a Proton run.

